# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = 'Song of Solomon'
TESTAMENT = 'Old Testament'
GENRE = 'poetry'
SUBTITLE = 'A Song of Love, Sought, Found, and Kept'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', 'The Song of Solomon opens, “The song of songs, which is Solomon’s” (Song 1:1). It is a poem of two lovers, with the daughters of Jerusalem as a chorus. It moves through longing and finding, spring and the seeking by night, the praise of the beloved, and ends with love set as a seal (Song 2:10; 5:16; 8:6–7).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'The book is introduced as Solomon’s song and addresses the daughters of Jerusalem, so its setting is Israelite (Song 1:1; 8:4). Its celebration of faithful love is also placed in the Universal Truths Table.')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of Song of Solomon', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how its message begins, how it develops, and how it builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — Longing and Finding (Chs. 1–2)'), ('p', 'The two lovers speak of one another, and the beloved calls, “Rise up, my love, my fair one, and come away” (Song 1:2; 2:10–12).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — Seeking and the Wedding (Chs. 3–4)'), ('p', 'The bride seeks her beloved, the wedding procession comes, and the bridegroom speaks of her (Song 4:9).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — Absence and Praise (Chs. 5–6)'), ('p', 'The beloved is sought and then praised as “altogether lovely” (Song 5:16; 6:3).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — Delight and Desire (Ch. 7)'), ('p', 'The beloved’s desire is toward her (Song 7:10).'), ('h3', 'Movement 5 — Love as a Seal (Ch. 8)'), ('p', 'Love is set as a seal, strong as death, and many waters cannot quench it (Song 8:4, 6–7).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in Song of Solomon', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how Israel should think and live under the covenants. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants Israel to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>A Song Within Israel</b> — “The song of songs, which is Solomon’s” (Song 1:1).', '<b>Mutual Belonging</b> — “My beloved is mine, and I am his” (Song 2:16).', '<b>The Call of the Beloved</b> — “Rise up, my love, my fair one, and come away” (Song 2:10).', '<b>Praise</b> — “He is altogether lovely” (Song 5:16).', '<b>Desire Returned</b> — “I am my beloved’s, and his desire is toward me” (Song 7:10).', '<b>Love Stronger Than Death</b> — “Love is strong as death” (Song 8:6).', '<b>Love Not Bought</b> — “Many waters cannot quench love, neither can the floods drown it” (Song 8:7).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in Song of Solomon', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — through foreshadowing, typology, and prophetic anticipation. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered trajectory before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these points together, you get a clear picture of how Song of Solomon points to Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>A Figure Not Named</b> — Some readers read the lovers as a figure of the LORD and Israel; the book does not say so, and it is reported as written.', '<b>Solomon the King</b> — The song is Solomon’s (Song 1:1); the Lord speaks of one greater than Solomon (Matt. 12:42).', '<b>No Quotation in the New Testament</b> — The New Testament does not quote the book, and no passage is built on it here.'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in Song of Solomon', [('p', 'This section helps you see how Song of Solomon speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. Song of Solomon operates within Israel’s prophetic framework. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read Song of Solomon in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('p', 'Song of Solomon reinforces Israel’s:'), ('ul', ['an Israelite setting and the daughters of Jerusalem (Song 1:1; 8:4)', 'the king’s song as part of Israel’s wisdom writings (Song 1:1)']), ('p', 'All content belongs exclusively to the Prophecy Program.')]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in Song of Solomon that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how Song of Solomon contributes to Israel’s story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['The Song of Songs — “The song of songs, which is Solomon’s.” (Song 1:1).', 'The Daughters of Jerusalem — “I charge you, O daughters of Jerusalem, that ye stir not up, nor awake my love, until he please.” (Song 8:4).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What Song of Solomon Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in Song of Solomon and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Israel’s covenant setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what Song of Solomon teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in Song of Solomon', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['The Song of Songs', '“The song of songs, which is Solomon’s.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Song 1:1'], ['The Daughters of Jerusalem', '“I charge you, O daughters of Jerusalem, that ye stir not up, nor awake my love, until he please.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Song 8:4']], [20, 34, 26, 20])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in Song of Solomon that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s covenant-specific instructions, this section helps you see what Song of Solomon teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['Love Declared', '“his banner over me was love.”', 'Song 2:4'], ['The Call', '“Rise up, my love, my fair one, and come away.”', 'Song 2:10'], ['Belonging', '“My beloved is mine, and I am his.”', 'Song 2:16'], ['The Heart Won', '“Thou hast ravished my heart, my sister, my spouse.”', 'Song 4:9'], ['Praise of the Beloved', '“he is altogether lovely.”', 'Song 5:16'], ['Desire Returned', '“I am my beloved’s, and his desire is toward me.”', 'Song 7:10'], ['Strong as Death', '“love is strong as death.”', 'Song 8:6'], ['Love Not Bought', '“Many waters cannot quench love, neither can the floods drown it.”', 'Song 8:7']], [28, 48, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of Song of Solomon without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s covenant story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to Song of Solomon in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Honour faithful love</b> — Love is strong as death (Song 8:6).', '<b>Belong to one another</b> — My beloved is mine, and I am his (Song 2:16).', '<b>Speak praise to the beloved</b> — He is altogether lovely (Song 5:16).', '<b>Answer desire with desire</b> — His desire is toward me (Song 7:10).', '<b>Know love cannot be bought</b> — Many waters cannot quench it (Song 8:7).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for Song of Solomon', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach Song of Solomon in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['Longing and Finding (Chs. 1–2) — The call of the beloved. <i>(Song 1:2; 2:10, 16)</i>', 'Seeking and the Wedding (Chs. 3–4) — The heart won. <i>(Song 4:9)</i>', 'Absence and Praise (Chs. 5–6) — Altogether lovely. <i>(Song 5:16)</i>', 'Delight and Desire (Ch. 7) — Desire returned. <i>(Song 7:10)</i>', 'Love as a Seal (Ch. 8) — Strong as death. <i>(Song 8:4, 6–7)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read Song of Solomon, helping you see both the prophetic structure of the book and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and universal truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward gratitude, faithfulness, and the worth of committed love without confusing Israel’s covenant commands with the Body of Christ’s calling today.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching Song of Solomon simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, prophecy-program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group distinguish Israel’s covenant identity from universal principles, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, Song of Solomon stands firmly in Israel’s covenant story, yet it still reveals universal truths that shape Christian character today. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s prophetic context while drawing out the timeless principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]

# Place in God's Storyline (Master Standard v1.155): last item of Section 1
from storyline import S as _STORY
